---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { PLAN_LIMITS } from '@golem/shared';

---

<Base
  title="Changelog — Apple"
  description="What shipped in Apple, release by release: engine changes, Plan and Agent, the Studio plugin, checkpoints, native undo and the Credits free tier."
>
  <section class="section clog" aria-labelledby="cl-title">
    <div class="wrap clog__wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Changelog</p>
      <h1 id="cl-title" class="carved">What shipped, in order</h1>
      <p class="lede lede-wide">
        Every release, what it actually does, no adjectives doing heavy lifting.
      </p>

      <ol class="releases">
        <!-- ------------------------------------------------ 2026-09-22 -->
        <li class="release" data-reveal aria-labelledby="rel-2026-09-22">
          <header class="release__head">
            <h2 id="rel-2026-09-22">
              <span class="release__tag mono">Update</span> The Studio plugin is on the Creator Store
            </h2>
            <p class="release__date mono">2026-09-22</p>
          </header>

          <p class="release__lede">
            Apple Studio, the plugin Apple builds through, can be installed from the Roblox Creator
            Store again, and it is free. <a href="/docs/plugin">Install the plugin</a> walks through
            getting it, enabling it, pairing it and allowing edits.
          </p>

          <h3>The public site</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>The site and the app now share one look, so moving between them feels like one product.</li>
            <li>Two modes, <strong>Plan</strong> and <strong>Agent</strong>, with Autonomous as a toggle on Agent. Apple and Apple MAX are models, not plans.</li>
          </ul>
        </li>

        <!-- ------------------------------------------------ v0.2 -->
        <li class="release" data-reveal aria-labelledby="rel-0-2">
          <header class="release__head">
            <h2 id="rel-0-2">
              <span class="release__tag mono">v0.2</span> One model, measured
            </h2>
            <p class="release__date mono">2026-08-30</p>
          </header>

          <p class="release__lede">
            Every mode moved onto a single model, chosen by the eval suite
            rather than by taste — and the migration made Apple both better and
            cheaper at once.
          </p>

          <h3>The engine</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              One engine now runs planning, building, memory and vision — 1M
              context, native tool calling, multimodal. Which engine that is, is
              an implementation detail Apple chooses per step; you never pick it
              and it can change without you noticing.
            </li>
            <li>
              Scored <strong>98.9</strong> on the Roblox eval suite against
              <strong>96.8</strong> for the model it replaced.
            </li>
            <li>
              A full Agent build fell from <strong>$0.0139 to $0.0056</strong> —
              2.5× cheaper for the same work, on a model that scores higher.
            </li>
            <li>
              Multi-model routing was <em>removed</em>. It existed to move cheap
              steps onto a smaller model; measurement showed that routing the
              post-inspection step changed the outcome for the worse — the small
              model read the project tree and declared the task finished instead
              of building it.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Spend control</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              The gateway now bills from the engine's own reported figure per call
              rather than from an estimate.
            </li>
            <li>
              Credits recalibrated: <strong>1 Credit = 30 neurons</strong>, so a
              Credit stays a meaningful unit now that the same work costs less.
            </li>
            <li>
              Four independent gates, each proven against production: a
              per-request ceiling, a daily ceiling, a monthly billable cap and an
              instant kill switch — all adjustable at runtime with no redeploy.
            </li>
            <li>
              Automatic retries removed entirely. A retry is a second bill for
              the same work.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Leaner context</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Tool results capped at 3,000 characters, agent transcript at 24,000.</li>
            <li>
              Tool definitions filtered per mode, so Plan does not pay to be told
              about playtesting tools.
            </li>
            <li>Run budgeting and metering were tightened while the agent loop was being stabilized.</li>
          </ul>
        </li>

        <!-- ------------------------------------------------ v0.1 -->
        <li class="release" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="90" aria-labelledby="rel-0-1">
          <header class="release__head">
            <h2 id="rel-0-1">
              <span class="release__tag mono">v0.1</span> First public release
            </h2>
            <p class="release__date mono">2026</p>
          </header>

          <p class="release__lede">
            Apple opens its doors. The full describe-to-build loop: a web
            workspace, a real Studio plugin, and an agent that changes your
            actual place — carefully.
          </p>

          <h3>The workspace</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Chat-driven building with live streaming responses and a visible
              tool trace — you see every <code>get_project_tree</code>,
              <code>edit_script</code> and <code>run_and_check</code> as it
              happens.
              <span class="release__since">
                Since narrowed: the independent Studio plugin never executes code it is sent, so
                <code>run_and_check</code> and the other tools that would need it are not offered
                while it is the plugin you have paired. The trace shows what actually ran. See
                <a href="/docs/plugin">the plugin docs</a> for the current refusals.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              The early work-mode controls were simplified as the product matured.
              <span class="release__since">
                Today Apple has <strong>Plan</strong> and <strong>Agent</strong>; Autonomous is a toggle
                on Agent. See <a href="/docs/modes">Modes</a> for the current behavior.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              Unlimited projects per account, each with its own chat history and
              memory of what you built.
            </li>
            <li>Image attachments — paste a screenshot straight into the chat.</li>
            <li>A stop button that actually stops the agent mid-run.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>The Studio plugin</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Pairing by short-lived code (<code>GLM-XXXXXX</code>, 10-minute
              expiry) — no tokens to copy around.
              <span class="release__since">
                Since changed: codes are six characters with no prefix, and the expiry is shown
                counting down in the pairing dialog.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>Reads and searches your scripts, edits them through Studio's own script editor APIs.</li>
            <li>Creates, moves, configures and deletes instances with typed, validated operations.</li>
            <li>
              Wraps every operation in its own ChangeHistory waypoint — native
              <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> undo, one step per change rather than one per run.
            </li>
            <li>
              Starts and stops run mode, captures logs, and reports results back for verification.
              <span class="release__since">
                Current implementation uses bounded Run-mode controls for Agent verification and can
                capture periodic viewport snapshots when Studio grants screenshot permission.
              </span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Safety net</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Automatic checkpoint before every agent run; manual checkpoints on demand.</li>
            <li>One-click restore of any checkpoint — scripts and instances — even across sessions.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>The deal</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Free tier: 60 Credits per day, hard quota, resets daily, no card ever collected.
              <span class="release__since">
                Since repriced: the free tier is {PLAN_LIMITS.free.creditsPerDay} Credits a day. A reader
                comparing this page with <a href="/pricing">Pricing</a> was seeing two different numbers
                for the same thing.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              Pro (400 Credits/day, priority queue, extended checkpoints) opens as a waitlist.
              <span class="release__since">
                Since withdrawn: there is no Pro tier. The paid tiers are Builder and Studio — see
                <a href="/pricing">Pricing</a>.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>Private projects are never used to train models.</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="clog__foot" data-reveal>
        Apple is in active development — expect the next entry sooner than you
        think.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .clog {
    position: relative;
    padding-top: clamp(48px, 7vw, 96px);
  }

  .clog__wrap {
    max-width: 840px;
  }

  /* A LIST, NOT A STACK OF CARDS (2026-09-22). Every release was a bordered panel, which is card
     soup on the one page that is nothing but entries. They are divided by a hairline now; the
     space between them does the separating. */
  .releases {
    display: grid;
    margin: clamp(32px, 5vw, 48px) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .release {
    padding: clamp(32px, 4.5vw, 48px) 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  .release__head {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 8px 16px;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }

  .release__head h2 {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 10px;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
  }

  .release__tag {
    padding: 2px 8px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-pill);
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 12px;
  }

  .release__date {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 12px;
  }

  .release__lede {
    max-width: 62ch;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .release__lede a,
  .release li a {
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--line-strong);
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  /* The note that corrects an old line: set apart by a hairline, so a reader can see at a glance
     which sentence is the record and which is the note about it. */
  .release__since {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 6px;
    padding-left: 12px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--line-strong);
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }

  .release h3 {
    margin: 28px 0 8px;
    color: var(--faint);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  /* Items are balanced on a phone: at 375px "pretty" still left "mid-run." alone on the last line
     of one (tests/rendered-typography.test.mjs). On a wide column balance halves short items, so
     it is pretty there. */
  .release ul {
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 20px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 15px;
  }

  .release li {
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .release li {
      text-wrap: balance;
    }
  }

  .release li::marker {
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  .release strong {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .clog__foot {
    margin-top: 40px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 15px;
  }
</style>
